WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.819 align:start position:0%
 
my first role-playing game was Chrono  Trigger I fell in love immediately for a  kid who hadn't yet seen Back to the  Future  squares time-traveling story was a  foreign concept I could battle dinosaurs  repair robots recruit anthropomorphic  frogs I could spend hours with chrono  and Luka prepping for those showdowns  with a parasite Lagos I could finish the  game more than a dozen different ways  all the while mesmerized by the links  between the past present and future  in short Chrono Trigger was a seminal  game for me its ever twisting plot and  colorful characters sparked an intense  love for RPGs specifically those made in  Japan I would later pour hours into  Suikoden - Secret of Mana Super Mario  RPG and most games in the Pokemon series  I love their long-form narratives I  loved grinding for experience  I loved the melodramatic sometimes  surrealistic world building  but as the videogame release schedule  became more crowded and the number of  quality games increased every year I  gradually fell off the JRPG train the  things I loved about the genre in the  first place became the very things that  pushed me away they began to feel too  long  granny began to seem like a waste of  time there's stories felt juvenile and  stacked against the likes of braid  Portal 2 or the Last of Us a magic I  discovered as a kid had all been  evaporated but just weeks ago it all  came rushing back persona 5 is hard to  define so I'll do my best to force it  into a nutshell it's equal parts social  simulator turn-based combat and high  school coming-of-age drama you spend  half of your time in the real world and  half of your time in the meta verse this  alternate reality that serves as the  physical manifestation of human thoughts  and emotions it's kind of like the  matrix and if you played a persona game  before then this is nothing new however  I haven't played a persona game before  this is all new to me I'm not usually  one for anime or high school dramas and  persona games were also notorious for  their length I was content to ignore  them for many of the reasons I ignored  JRPGs for the last 10 years or so yet  for some reason I began persona 5 maybe  it was a jazz infused soundtrack maybe  it was the assertive style it exudes at  every turn regardless it reminded me of  my first time playing chrono trigger all  those years ago after only a few hours I  knew this is a game I can fall into its  world envelops me it's combat entices me  its narrative pulls me in the dichotomy  between its stylized version of Tokyo  and the psychological Metaverse creates  intriguing plot points and nuanced  character studies and it propels both  forward at a confident pace  what's more persona 5 spends much of its  time setting up expectations only to  shatter them down the line it also  suffers most of the JRPG tropes I've  grown tired of over the years it all  begins with the combat as with several  other games in the series persona 5 lets  you negotiate with your enemies during  holdups after you've discerned their  weaknesses and exploited them as well  this not only changes the flow of combat  but it also allows you to absorb the  enemy's power for later use as you  gather more persone  throughout the game negotiations also  ask you to participate intense  conversations with these enemies you  have to read them you have to flirt  insult argue and comfort you tell some  what they want to hear and others what  they don't persona 5 asked you to engage  in verbal jiu-jitsu and it's just as  rewarding as the combat and many other  JRPGs but persona 5 is disruptive on  higher levels - more than anything this  game respects my time it takes more than  100 hours to beat and somehow almost  everyone of them feels integral to the  experience this is largely because of  how persona 5 actually deals with time  many of your missions have deadlines  attached giving you only a couple of  weeks to prepare for your next trek into  the Metaverse where you'll carry out a  psychological heist to give each villain  a real world change of heart  while these time limits could be  damaging in a lesser game persona 5  wields them with expertise gathering  supplies experienced and useful  supporting characters could normally be  a grind towards some arbitrary goal but  there's a certain urgency here that  lends everything more weight with a  looming deadline to consider grinding  becomes a thrill  and that's the thing for sort of five  makes the grind fun it makes it  meaningful when I turn off the game for  the day and I think about my progress I  don't feel like any of my time was  wasted because persona 5 knows time is  important and it's built around that  idea to see this in any game is  impressive but to see in a game this  length is astounding that is each and  every small activity feels important  within the larger game exploring Tokyo's  nightlife improves your social skills  doing laundry cleans valuable equipment  studying increases your knowledge which  aids your relationship with intelligent  characters which grants you new combat  skills and dialogue options and then  there are the relationships themselves  by earning the confidence of your fellow  students and Tokyo citizens you'll build  a ragtag band of thieves and accomplices  gaining notoriety and power in both the  real world and the Metaverse leaving  aside the fantastic dialogue and  heartfelt nature of persona 5 social  aspects it's also just downright fun to  foster these relationships you learn  your allies on personal and functional  levels you gain new skills as you  improve these bonds you form a psychic  army of sorts to take on Tokyo's corrupt  underbelly first one of five social  aspects don't sit apart from its combat  in exploration it's all of a piece what  kind of person do you think I was  there's no way I'd turn out to be some  bad guy in the end right and although  I'm only just beginning to talk about  the characters it's actually here we're  persona 5 is at its most brilliant RPG  characters have this tendency to fall  into categories filling out arc types in  the service of a familiar structure from  game to game  there's the brash rebellious Punk the  antisocial geek the just rule abiding  citizen persona 5 has each of these but  as in the rest of the game it jumps at  the chance to pick these stereotypes  apart just when I thought I knew a  character they revealed something about  their past or their life at home and it  warped my perception in many cases the  game showed how someone might mold their  personality to fit these archetypes  on purpose it's no coincidence that this  unfolds during these characters high  school years it's a time when labels run  rampant amplifying the confusion of  people who don't know who they are or  where they're going the characters and  persona 5 embody this confusion in  spades they're all outsiders they're all  lonely and they deal with it in  different ways through violence  rebellion withdrawal or are  for such a stylistic melodramatic game  persona 5 can be painfully surprisingly  human like its predecessors this is a  game about identity and how its  characters suppress there's only to see  it manifest in the form of powerful  personas in the Metaverse and as they  grow in that psychological world they  grow in the real world too they begin to  express themselves they gain some  semblance of understanding as they  undergo trials and build relationships  I've started several JRPGs in recent  years hoping one of them would pull me  back into the genre I fell in love with  so long ago but the more I tried the  more I felt like giving up it felt like  I was searching for something that  wasn't there yet my favorite games and  movies deconstruct the genres they're  placed in and here is a game that does  just that persona 5 examines the  character archetypes combat systems and  repetitive grinding often attributed to  the JRPG space and flies in the face of  each and every one like its rebellious  subversive characters persona 5 defies  the labels its surface value attracts  [Music]  [Laughter]  there's been a slew of great JRPGs in  the year since I stopped pouring time  into them but like Chrono Trigger all  those years ago persona 5 sparked a  fascination I can't easily ignore it  reminded me why I fell in love with  JRPGs in the first place and I can't  wait to go back and see what I've missed  [Music]  hey everyone thanks so much for watching  the fourth episode of reboot I really  enjoyed talking about persona 5 and Jake  and I really enjoyed doing this show it  helps us a lot when you get your  comments in on youtube and tweet at us  giving us questions comments concerns so  in the meantime when we talk about  persona 5 feedback on next episode get  those comments in and we will also tease  the next episode we'll see you that  [Music]

